  proved to be a \"brown tumor\". The scan showed the typical findings of renal osteodystrophy, but also a diffuse      
  extra-skeletal uptake of bone tracer in the lungs, kidneys, femoral arteries and myocardium. After surgery, good      
  control of serum calcium, phosphate (Ca x P product < 50 mg2/dl2) and PTH levels was maintained during 4 years of     
  follow-up. Bone scans were repeated after 2 and 4 years, showing marked improvement of periarticular uptake at the    
  ends of long bones. Extraosseous calcium deposition was still markedly evident, but progressively decreased (at 4     
  years: heart -36%, lungs -18%). CONCLUSION In this dialysis patient, extraskeletal calcification of visceral organs   
